\begin{abstract}
%TL;DR of paragraph: the field evaluates 3D structure by rendering and embedding, and that protocol cannot see interiors.
Generative models of discrete {3D} structure --- LEGO assemblies, voxel
buildings, block worlds --- are evaluated by rendering a few views, embedding
them with a pretrained encoder, and measuring a distribution distance to real
data. We show that this protocol cannot see whether a structure has an inside.

%TL;DR of paragraph: the headline measurement and the four ways we show it is the representation, not one metric.
Filling every sealed room in a corpus of real buildings --- a third more blocks,
the exterior unchanged --- moves a multi-view DINOv2 kernel distance by $0.38$
of its own sampling noise at $n{=}128$. A distance computed on voxel descriptors
instead moves it by more than $200$ at the same $n$. This is not a quirk of one metric or one
encoder: across two domains, all seven render-space statistics we test miss the
corruption while catching every other one we apply, and no encoder (CLIP,
ResNet-50, the Inception network FID uses) and no view budget up to sixteen
views does better. Rendering cutaway views recovers the signal with the same
encoder and the same statistic, which locates the failure in the view protocol.
Under an opaque renderer the blindness is not statistical but \emph{exact}:
filling sealed voids cannot change any exterior view, so no encoder, statistic
or view budget can ever separate it, and we verify bit-identical renders on
every held-out build. Real renderers leak a little --- translucent blocks let
the fill show, altering $91\%$ of views by up to the full dynamic range in the
pixels it touches --- and every statistic in this family discards even that.
Hollowing a structure is equally invisible, so the blindness runs in both
directions.

%TL;DR of paragraph: our prescription is to validate metrics against known corruptions first, and it cost us our own headline result.
We argue that a metric should be validated against known corruptions before it
ranks models, and release a suite that does this: blocking ordering, invariance
and sample-size gates, complementary render and geometric measures, and explicit
disqualification for memorization and mode collapse. Applying it to our own
models overturned the headline result of this project: a generator with $87\%$
test contamination lost its apparent advantage over the baselines once retrained
on a clean split.
\end{abstract}
